    tldr: "Every radioactive treatment leaves behind radioactive waste, such as used vials and, for a short time, the patient's own urine, which has to be safely stored until it stops being radioactive. The rules and the physical space needed for this differ from hospital to hospital and country to country, and building enough of that safe storage takes time.",
    summary:
      "US NRC rule 10 CFR 35.92 allows medical licensees to hold low-level radioactive waste for 'decay-in-storage' -- keeping it until a survey shows its radiation level is indistinguishable from background, then disposing of it as ordinary or medical waste -- but only for byproduct material with a physical half-life under 120 days. Most therapeutic isotopes qualify (lutetium-177's 6.65-day half-life decays in weeks), but contaminants complicate this: NRC guidance on Lutathera and Pluvicto notes that the metastable isotope lutetium-177m, with a 161-day half-life, can be present at roughly 0.02% of total activity and, when detected, must instead be handled as formal low-level radioactive waste under 10 CFR Part 20, a different and more burdensome disposal route.\n\nLow-level radioactive waste itself is classified by the NRC into Classes A, B and C by hazard; Class A, the least hazardous, makes up about 96% of total LLRW volume. A UK health-system readiness review lists radioactive-waste management -- decay-tank capacity, shielded 'hot' toilets for patients who have received a therapeutic dose, and staying within site-specific disposal limits -- as one of five mandatory steps any facility must have in place before it can deliver radioligand therapy at all, alongside licensing, imaging, treatment planning and follow-up.\n\nRegulatory fragmentation adds friction on top of the physical constraint: in the US, medical radioactive-material licensing is split between the NRC directly and 39 'Agreement States' that have taken on their own licensing authority, so requirements are not identical nationwide, and a site opening a new radioligand therapy programme must navigate whichever regime applies locally, often adding months to programme start-up alongside the physical build-out of shielded waste storage.",
